[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Interpolation Search"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interpolation search is a search algorithm used to find a specific element within a sorted array. It improves upon binary search by estimating the position of the target value based on its value relative to the values at the beginning and end of the search interval. This makes it particularly effective for uniformly distributed data."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Algorithm Overview"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The core idea behind interpolation search is to estimate the position of the target element using the following formula:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"pos = low + ((target - arr[low]) * (high - low)) / (arr[high] - arr[low])"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Where:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"pos is the estimated position of the target element."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"low is the index of the lower bound of the search interval."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"high is the index of the upper bound of the search interval."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"target is the value being searched for."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"arr is the sorted array."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Algorithm Steps"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The interpolation search algorithm can be summarized in the following steps:"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. Initialize low to 0 and high to the last index of the array (arr.length - 1)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. While low is less than or equal to high and the target is within the range of values in the array (arr[low] <= target <= arr[high]):"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. Calculate the estimated position (pos) using the formula: pos = low + ((target - arr[low]) * (high - low)) / (arr[high] - arr[low])."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"4. If arr[pos] is equal to the target, return pos."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"5. If arr[pos] is less than the target, update low to pos + 1 (search in the right subarray)."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"6. If arr[pos] is greater than the target, update high to pos - 1 (search in the left subarray)."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"7. If the target is not found after the loop terminates, return -1."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Example Implementation (Java)"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"public class InterpolationSearch {
    public static int interpolationSearch(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0, high = arr.length - 1;

        while (low <= high && target >= arr[low] && target <= arr[high]) {
            if (low == high) {
                if (arr[low] == target) return low;
                return -1;
            }

            int pos = low + ((target - arr[low]) * (high - low)) / (arr[high] - arr[low]);

            if (arr[pos] == target)
                return pos;

            if (arr[pos] < target)
                low = pos + 1;
            else
                high = pos - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {10, 12, 13, 16, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 33, 35, 42, 47};
        int target = 22;
        int index = interpolationSearch(arr, target);

        if (index != -1)
            System.out.println("Element found at index " + index);
        else
            System.out.println("Element not found.");
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Time Complexity"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"The time complexity of interpolation search depends on the distribution of the data. In the best case (uniformly distributed data), the time complexity is O(log log n). In the worst case (data is not uniformly distributed), the time complexity can degrade to O(n)."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Space Complexity"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"The space complexity of interpolation search is O(1) as it only requires a constant amount of extra space."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Advantages"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Can be faster than binary search for uniformly distributed data."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Disadvantages"},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Performance degrades significantly for non-uniformly distributed data."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Requires the data to be sorted."},{"order":32,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interpolation search is most suitable when searching for elements in a sorted array where the data is known to be uniformly distributed. If the data distribution is unknown or non-uniform, binary search is generally a safer and more reliable choice."}]
